  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15B in contracts in FY2026 to date, covering 21,855 awards with an average award size of $686K. Obligations fell 29.5% year over year. The department's current-year total is partial, representing fiscal year to date activity rather than a full-year total. Historical annual obligations show $34.3B in FY21, $33.4B in FY22, $33.4B in FY23, $29.9B in FY24, and $21.3B in FY25. Computer and consulting services are the largest spending categories in FY2026 to date, led by Computer Systems Design Services at $2.5B, Other Computer Related Services at $1.3B, and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $1.3B. Custom Computer Programming Services accounts for $1B. Research and development and medical-sector lines also appear in the top ten, with Research and Development in Biotechnology at $941M, Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $876M, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $582M. The top vendors list mixes large IT and consulting firms, health services suppliers, and nonprofit contractors, led by GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $741M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What is driving the year-over-year fall in obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15B in FY2026 to date, down 29.5% year over year. The department recorded FY25 obligations of $21.3B and earlier fiscal-year totals as high as $34.3B. A large share of FY2026 to date dollars sits in IT and professional services categories, including multiple NAICS lines with values above $1B. The partial status of FY2026 to date means total obligations will change as the fiscal year continues."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is HHS concentrating FY2026 contract dollars?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the single largest NAICS category at $2.5B. The department also obligated $1.3B each to Other Computer Related Services and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services, and $1B to Custom Computer Programming Services. Research and medical categories are significant as well, with $941M for Biotechnology R&D, $876M for physical and life sciences R&D, and $582M for Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Health and Human Services spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The department issued 21,855 awards at an average award size of $686K, and obligations are down 29.5% year over year. Fiscal trends show prior-year totals of $34.3B in FY21, $33.4B in FY22, $33.4B in FY23, $29.9B in FY24, and $21.3B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Health and Human Services buy?",
      "a": "HHS buys a lot of IT and professional services, with Computer Systems Design Services at $2.5B, Other Computer Related Services at $1.3B, Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $1.3B, and Custom Computer Programming Services at $1B. HHS also buys research and development services, including Research and Development in Biotechnology at $941M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $876M, plus categories like Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $730M and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $582M."
    },
